    "source": "DOCS/CHANGELOG.md",
    "present": true,
    "heading": "v1.37.82443 — 2026-08-17",
    "highlights": [
      "The Mass Map is now ✠ **Holy Mass** in the rail, and the map view carries its own content types: **Scriptura** lays the whole canon out as two subway lines — hover a book stop and its chapters open right there on the stop; click a chapter to read. **Horæ** runs the day's eight hours as an office line — hover for the hour's parts, click to pray it.",
      "The loud pill that the global button skin painted behind active strip stops is gone; the subtle pulsating you-are-here ring breathes alone again.",
      "The map remembers its content type when you leave and come back through the sidebar.",
      "Book chips on the scripture strip open their chapter menu on hover, right on the stop.",
      "🔖 Annotations moves to the end of the rail, with your other writing surfaces.",
      "The deuterocanonical books regain their Latin (Tobias, Wisdom, Baruch now fully bilingual from the Clementine tradition; Judith and Ecclesiasticus carry it across their aligned chapters)."
    ],
    "markdown": "The map becomes three maps — Mass, Scripture, and the Hours — and the lost Latin of the deuterocanonical books returns.\n\n- The Mass Map is now ✠ **Holy Mass** in the rail, and the map view carries its own content types: **Scriptura** lays the whole canon out as two subway lines — hover a book stop and its chapters open right there on the stop; click a chapter to read. **Horæ** runs the day's eight hours as an office line — hover for the hour's parts, click to pray it.\n- The loud pill that the global button skin painted behind active strip stops is gone; the subtle pulsating you-are-here ring breathes alone again.\n- The map remembers its content type when you leave and come back through the sidebar.\n- Book chips on the scripture strip open their chapter menu on hover, right on the stop.\n- 🔖 Annotations moves to the end of the rail, with your other writing surfaces.\n- The deuterocanonical books regain their Latin (Tobias, Wisdom, Baruch now fully bilingual from the Clementine tradition; Judith and Ecclesiasticus carry it across their aligned chapters)."
  },
